<TopicPage slug="subjects/fashion-design/foundational/fashion-design-research">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Fashion design begins before sketching: with research. A collection is an argument about the
    world — a mood, a silhouette, a palette — and the designer's first job is to gather, curate
    and transform the raw material that argument needs. Think of it like cooking: nobody opens a
    restaurant by writing the menu first. They taste, shop, and find what the season offers; the
    menu is the last, shortest step. Designers who skip research are writing menus from an empty
    pantry, and the result is the same in both trades — generic dishes that taste of nothing in
    particular.
  </p>
  <p>
    The research book is the industry's most revealing artefact. Show a designer's book and you
    can predict the collection: the mood pages become the palette, the texture pages become the
    fabric list, the structural studies become the seams. This is why tutors, juries and
    design directors read books so closely — the book shows the thinking, and thinking is what
    they are hiring. A sketch on its own is an opinion; a research book is the evidence behind it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Research also answers a question every designer eventually faces: why should this collection
    exist? Clothes are made by the millions every season, and most are forgettable. The collections
    that matter come from a designer who has looked hard at something — a subculture, a historical
    decade, a material behaviour — and has something to say about it. Research is how a designer
    finds that something to say.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from reference to collection: the research pipeline" widget="equationStep" config={researchCfg} />

  <h2>Research books and mood</h2>
  <p>
    A research book (sometimes called a sketchbook, journal or workbook) is the designer's primary
    working document. It is part scrapbook, part laboratory notebook: photographs, fabric swatches,
    colour chips, postcards, magazine tears, pressed leaves, own drawings and handwritten notes,
    arranged so that a stranger could follow the argument. Books can be physical — preferred at
    most design schools because fabric handles and colour read truer in person — or digital boards,
    but the discipline is identical.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most important distinction in gathering is <strong>primary versus secondary</strong> research.
    Primary research is material you generated or experienced yourself: photographs you took at a
    vintage market, rubbings from a manhole cover, a scarf you bought and unravelled to see how it
    was knitted, notes from an archive visit. Secondary research is material someone else made:
    images from books and museums, historical plates, film stills, online archives. Both are
    legitimate, but primary research is prized because it is unrepeatable — no other designer in
    the world has your photographs, your swatches, your observations. A book of only secondary
    images reads like a Pinterest board; a book with primary material reads like a designer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Curation is the second half of the skill. A strong book is an <em>argument, not an archive</em>:
    every image earns its place by supporting the developing idea. In practice this means editing
    ruthlessly — a degree-level book for one project typically runs tens of pages, not hundreds,
    and each spread is composed deliberately, like a magazine editor composes a story. Alongside
    the book, designers build <strong>moodboards</strong>: single compressed surfaces holding the
    palette, key textures, silhouette references and atmosphere words, so the whole mood can be
    absorbed in one glance. The moodboard is the collection's contract with itself — when a
    sketch later drifts off-mood, the board is what pulls it back.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> more images means more research. In reality, thirty images that
    are all responding to each other beat three hundred collected indiscriminately. Research is
    judged by depth of engagement — drawing over a photograph, testing a colour, taking a garment
    apart — not by volume of clippings.
  </p>

  <h2>Concept development</h2>
  <p>
    Concept development is the step where gathered material becomes a controlling idea. A concept
    is the collection's thesis, and it should be sayable in one sentence: "workwear deconstructed
    for the city", "a wardrobe for a future where water is rationed", "Edwardian mourning dress
    rebuilt in technical knit". The test of a good concept is that it keeps making decisions for
    you — it suggests silhouettes, excludes certain colours, points at particular fabrics. When a
    designer cannot say their concept in a sentence, every later decision becomes an argument
    with themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    The heart of concept development is <strong>transformation</strong>: copying a reference is
    illustration; abstracting one is design. The discipline's classic exercise is to take a
    non-fashion image — a beetle's shell, a brutalist stairwell, a 1920s photogram — and translate
    it into structure, seam and surface. Concretely: a photograph of overlapping chapel windows
    might become a bodice whose panels overlap without meeting, the leading becoming piping, the
    coloured glass becoming a shaded colour gradient across the panels. The window is never printed
    on the cloth; it is rebuilt as construction. Judges and tutors read the distance between
    reference and outcome — too close is pastiche, too far is incoherence — and the sweet spot in
    between is where a designer's handwriting appears.
  </p>
  <p>
    Concept development also means designing the <strong>line-up</strong>, not just individual
    garments. A professional women's or menswear collection is an ensemble: shapes that talk to
    each other, a deliberate balance of statement pieces and commercial ones. A typical
    twelve-look student capsule might hold two or three statement looks that carry the concept at
    full volume, six or seven core looks where the idea is worn daily, and a couple of
    commercial closes that a store could actually stock. Research is where this coherence is
    born — the mood board fixes the palette, the fabric board fixes the
    materials, and every look is checked against both. Drag the widget's pipeline through its
    five stages and the sequence is the unit's argument in miniature: gather widely, curate
    with intent, transform, fix the colour and fabric story, then design for the line-up.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> staying "faithful" to the reference by copying it closely is a
    virtue. It is the opposite — visible copying is the one failure every critique names first.
    The reference is fuel, not a template; the design is what you do to it.
  </p>

  <h2>Fabric is the design's co-author</h2>
  <p>
    Silhouettes are negotiations with material. A liquid satin wants the bias cut, where the cloth
    falls diagonally across its grain and drapes like poured water; a stiff duchess satin builds
    architecture, holding a sculpted shoulder or a bell-shaped skirt on its own; a technical knit
    shapes itself, stretching over the body without a single dart. The same sketch executed in
    three fabrics is three different garments — which is why professional practice is
    <em>drape-first</em>: pin cloth on the stand early, let its behaviour edit the sketch, and
    treat the fabric board as half the collection's identity.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fabric story is locked during research, well before final sketches. Designers gather
    swatches — small samples of every candidate cloth — and sort them by hand-feel, weight,
    stretch and how they take colour. Students who sketch without swatches design garments that
    cannot exist: a collar that no available cloth will hold, a pleat that a heavy coating would
    crack at the fold. Fabric is not decoration applied at the end; it is the physical argument
    the whole collection is made of.
  </p>

  <h2>Design history and reference</h2>
  <p>
    Every silhouette returns. The 1920s dropped the waist and shortened the skirt to liberate
    movement; Dior's 1947 New Look cinched the waist and poured out the skirt as a deliberate
    return to opulence after wartime rationing; the 1990s minimalists stripped everything back to
    the slip dress and the bias cut. Because the archive cycles, the designer who knows it knows
    what the present is quoting. A designer looking at today's runways without historical
    knowledge sees novelty everywhere; one who knows the century sees revivals, reactions and
    remixes with named sources.
  </p>
  <p>
    Subcultures are the other great reference library. Punk's safety pins and DIY deconstruction
    travelled from 1970s streets to Vivienne Westwood's runways and then into mainstream leather
    jackets forever; hip-hop's oversized silhouettes, sportswear and logos reshaped menswear
    entirely. Designers research subculture because it is where clothing meaning is invented
    before the industry borrows it — and because the borrowings have histories worth knowing
    before repeating.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical payoff is precision. Research into fashion history, subculture and the global
    industry is not academic decoration; it is the difference between proposing something new and
    unknowingly reprising something old. A graduate presenting "puffed shoulders and nipped waists"
    as a fresh idea will meet a jury member who quietly notes it is 1984. The same student who
    knows the 1984 power suit, knows why it meant authority then, and can articulate what it
    should mean now has turned reference into authorship.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "everything has already been done, so history means nothing is
    new." The useful reading is the reverse: because silhouettes return, novelty lives in the
    <em>reasoning</em> — new fabrics, new contexts, new wearers for old shapes. History is the
    vocabulary the present speaks with an accent.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    In industry, research operates inside a calendar and a brief. Collections are designed
    twelve to eighteen months ahead of delivery, so "research into the present" is partly
    systematised through trend agencies such as WGSN and colour systems such as Pantone, which
    publish season-ahead forecasts that much of the industry then reads — an odd loop where
    prediction shapes the future it predicts. Designer education deliberately breaks this loop:
    school projects usually write their own briefs from primary research precisely so students
    learn to generate ideas the forecasts did not hand them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Research books are also assessed as craft objects, with conventions worth learning early.
    Juries look for a visible journey: early pages broad and exploratory, middle pages narrowing
    through drawing and development, final pages resolving into flats, fabric boards and a colour
    story. Annotation matters because it externalises judgement — a note like "this drape flattens
    the silhouette; try the crisper poplin" shows decision-making, which is the thing being
    graded. Photocollage, traced silhouettes over references, fabric-manipulation samples taped
    to the page and colour chips pulled from photographs are all standard research verbs; pasting
    alone is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two further layers deepen professional practice. First, <strong>user and market research</strong>:
    commercial design adds who-wears-this analysis — price points, size ranges, care expectations —
    so the line-up's balance of statement and commercial pieces is evidence-based rather than
    guessed. Second, <strong>ethical sourcing as research</strong>: interrogating where a fabric
    comes from, what a print's cultural symbols mean to their originators, and whether deadstock
    or zero-waste construction could carry the concept is now core research, not an add-on —
    several of the most-cited graduate collections of recent years were built on the question
    "what if this collection generated no cutting-room waste?" The research book, in other words,
    is where a designer decides what kind of designer to be.
  </p>

  <p>
    A note on the book's afterlife: research does not end at the line-up. The best books are
    revisited — pages return as source material for later collections, colour stories get
    re-tested against new fabrics, and a designer's own archive becomes their private
    museum. Treat every project's research as an investment in the next ten years of
    authorship, not a disposable worksheet for one grade.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> UAL / London College of Fashion programme model; Sorger &amp; Udale, <em>The Fundamentals of Fashion Design</em>; Quinn, <em>Fashion Futures</em>.</p>
